      <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Complete Guide · 2026</p>
        <h1>Trespasse in Lisbon:<br />the complete guide to buying or selling a business</h1>

        <div class="byline">
          <div class="byline-avatar">G</div>
          <div>
            <div class="byline-name">Grégory Vincent</div>
            <div class="byline-role">Commercial Agent · Former Lisbon business owner</div>
          </div>
          <span class="byline-read">12 min read</span>
        </div>

        <p class="lead">
          If you're looking to buy or sell a business in Lisbon, you'll encounter the word <strong>trespasse</strong> everywhere.
          It's the Portuguese legal mechanism for transferring a going concern — and it's very different from buying a company outright.
          Understanding how it works is the first step to getting the transaction right.
        </p>

        <h2 id="def">1. What is a trespasse?</h2>

        <p>
          A <strong>trespasse</strong> (pronounced treh-PASS) is the transfer of a business establishment as a going concern.
          The buyer takes over the operation as it runs: the commercial lease, equipment, stock, operating licences,
          supplier contracts, and customer base.
        </p>

        <p>
          What does <em>not</em> transfer: the legal entity (the Portuguese lda or sa), its tax history, and its past debts.
          The seller keeps the company. The buyer takes over the activity — typically through their own newly formed entity.
        </p>

        <div class="hl">
          <p>
            <strong>Key distinction:</strong> in a <em>share deal</em> (cession of quotas or shares), the buyer purchases the entire company —
            including any historic liabilities. In a trespasse, they acquire only the operation. For most retail businesses, restaurants,
            cafés, and shops in Lisbon, the trespasse is the simpler and better-protected route for both parties.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Legal basis: Article 1113.º of the Portuguese Civil Code, the commercial lease law (NRAU), and Article 285.º
          of the Labour Code governing employee transfers.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contenu">2. What the buyer takes over</h2>

        <h3>The commercial lease</h3>
        <p>
          The lease transfers to the buyer <strong>without prior landlord consent</strong> — but the landlord
          <strong>must be notified within 15 days</strong> of the transfer. If that notification is missed, the transfer is
          unenforceable against the landlord and can become grounds for lease termination. In the case of a sale (as opposed to
          a gratuitous transfer), the landlord also holds a <strong>right of pre-emption</strong>.
        </p>

        <h3>Employees</h3>
        <p>
          Employment contracts transfer <strong>automatically</strong> to the buyer, with full seniority and acquired rights intact
          (Art. 285.º Labour Code). The buyer is jointly liable for wages owed in the <strong>6 months preceding</strong> the transfer.
        </p>

        <h3>Operating licences</h3>
        <p>
          This is the most critical point to verify before signing anything. Some licences are attached to the premises
          (ASAE alcohol licence, terrace licence) and transfer with the business. Others are <strong>personal</strong> —
          the new operator must apply for them from scratch, which takes time.
        </p>

        <h3>Equipment, stock and brand</h3>
        <p>Everything that makes up the operational asset, as listed in the inventory schedule attached to the transfer contract.</p>

        <h2 id="processus">3. The transaction process step by step</h2>

        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">1</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Business valuation</h3>
              <p>Value is based on EBITDA (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation), adjusted for residual rent, remaining lease term, and equipment condition. See the valuation section below.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">2</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Mandate and confidential marketing</h3>
              <p>The agent publishes a blind teaser — sector and key metrics, without naming the business. Confidentiality protects the operation and staff throughout the sale process.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">3</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Buyer due diligence</h3>
              <p>Access to 2–3 years of accounts, the commercial lease, licences, and equipment inventory. This is the moment to verify the assignment clause in the lease, any employee liabilities, and licence transferability.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">4</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Heads of agreement (Contrato-Promessa)</h3>
              <p>Sets the price, conditions precedent, and completion date. Signed with a deposit — typically 10% of the transfer price.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">5</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Transfer deed</h3>
              <p>Private deed or notarised deed depending on the assets involved. The landlord is notified within 15 days. Balance of the purchase price is paid at signing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-n">6</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Operational handover</h3>
              <p>Transfer of utility contracts, supplier accounts, and obtaining licences in the new operator's name. Joint inventory of stock and equipment at handover.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hl">
          <p><strong>Total timeline: 30 to 90 days</strong> depending on complexity. For a straightforward deal with clean documentation, the average is 45 to 60 days.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="valorisation">4. Valuation: what is a Lisbon business worth?</h2>

        <p>Value is driven by <strong>EBITDA</strong> — what the business actually generates after variable costs, before rent and depreciation — not by gross revenue. Typical multiples observed in Lisbon:</p>

        <div class="data-table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Business type</th><th>EBITDA multiple</th><th>Prime location premium</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Restaurant / café / bar</td><td class="accent-cell">1.0× – 2.5×</td><td>+15 to 30%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Retail / shop</td><td class="accent-cell">0.5× – 1.5×</td><td>+10 to 20%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Services (salon, wellness…)</td><td class="accent-cell">0.5× – 1.2×</td><td>+10 to 15%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Concept / atypical</td><td class="accent-cell">Case by case</td><td>Depends on brand</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Prime locations (Chiado, Baixa, Príncipe Real, Cais do Sodré, Alfama, LX Factory) command significant premiums.
          A <strong>below-market historic rent</strong> — common on older Lisbon leases — is a major asset and should be reflected
          directly in the valuation.
        </p>

        <div class="hl">
          <p><strong>A beautiful but empty business is worth its fixtures, nothing more.</strong> Valuation always starts from actual trading figures, never from what the seller invested.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="fiscalite">5. Taxation</h2>

        <h3>For the seller</h3>
        <p>The capital gain on a business transfer is subject to Portuguese IRS (category B income or professional capital gains depending on the structure). Allowances may apply based on the holding period. Pre-sale tax structuring is strongly recommended.</p>

        <h3>For the buyer</h3>
        <p>A trespasse is generally <strong>exempt from VAT</strong> when it constitutes a transfer of a going concern (Art. 3.º(4) CIVA). <strong>IMT</strong> (property transfer tax) and <strong>Stamp Duty</strong> (Imposto de Selo) may apply if real property rights are included. The value of acquired assets can be depreciated for corporate tax purposes.</p>

        <p class="fiscal-note">This is provided for information only. Consult a qualified Portuguese tax adviser for your specific situation.</p>

        <h2 id="erreurs">6. The 6 most common mistakes</h2>

        <div class="warn-grid">
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">1</div><h3>Missing the assignment clause</h3><p>Some older leases prohibit or restrict assignment. Check before making any offer.</p></div>
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">2</div><h3>Ignoring the landlord's pre-emption</h3><p>The landlord holds a right of pre-emption in a sale. This period must be factored into the timeline.</p></div>
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">3</div><h3>Underestimating employee liabilities</h3><p>A 10-year employee represents a significant potential future cost. Understand this before signing.</p></div>
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">4</div><h3>Paying for the seller's dreams</h3><p>The market pays for today's EBITDA, not for past renovation costs or unrealised potential.</p></div>
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">5</div><h3>Non-transferable licences</h3><p>Some licences are personal — the new operator must obtain them fresh, which takes time and may require specific qualifications.</p></div>
          <div class="warn-card"><div class="warn-num">6</div><h3>No financing in place</h3><p>Bank financing for a trespasse requires 30–50% equity. Arrange this before making an offer.</p></div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq">7. Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Does the landlord have to agree to a trespasse?</div><p class="faq-a">No prior consent is required. But the landlord must be notified within 15 days of the transfer. If this is missed, the trespasse is unenforceable against the landlord and can trigger lease termination.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">How long does a trespasse transaction in Lisbon take?</div><p class="faq-a">Between 30 and 90 days depending on complexity — number of employees, type of licences, accounting documentation. For a straightforward deal with clean books, the average is 45 to 60 days.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Can a trespasse be financed by a bank loan?</div><p class="faq-a">Yes, though it is more demanding than a property mortgage. Portuguese banks (CGD, Millennium, Santander) do finance some business transfers with personal guarantees. Expect to need 30–50% equity minimum, and prepare a solid dossier: 3 years of accounts, a business plan, and your operating CV.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">What happens to employees if I don't want to keep them all?</div><p class="faq-a">Contracts transfer automatically with full seniority. If you wish to let someone go, you must follow the Portuguese Labour Code — notice period and redundancy payment calculated on actual length of service.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Can a foreigner buy a trespasse in Portugal?</div><p class="faq-a">Yes, without restriction. You will need a Portuguese NIF (tax identification number), a Portuguese bank account, and a local legal entity (typically a lda) to operate the business. These steps run in parallel with the transaction and take 2–4 weeks.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">What is the difference between a trespasse and buying a company (share deal)?</div><p class="faq-a">In a trespasse, you acquire the business activity but not the legal entity — meaning you don't inherit past debts or tax history. In a share deal, you buy the entire company, including all its historic liabilities. For most standard businesses in Lisbon, the trespasse is the cleaner and more common route.</p></div>
          <div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-q">Who pays the agent's fee?</div><p class="faq-a">The commission is paid by the seller — the buyer pays nothing to the agent. It covers the marketing, buyer qualification, negotiation, and full coordination through to signing.</p></div>
        </div>

